3/selection/ma-v1/ae1030de8cc55eaa89a7ff76b335d62f755394361c50f0224f5c7013158a0390/0112f78b443cd401e4d0e83cb3b567685c157bc6e858b9f478baff3b065df996" :answer/id #uuid "a41807ba-6bc5-5c5a-9f9d-cea9dffbd0f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76473/selection/ma-v1/ae1030de8cc55eaa89a7ff76b335d62f755394361c50f0224f5c7013158a0390" :answer-field/id #uuid "c83a343d-c250-5272-8bb1-febadff806e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-76473/selection/ma-v1/ae1030de8cc55eaa89a7ff76b335d62f755394361c50f0224f5c7013158a0390/b331b72fbc647af25e87beeed0ecd2094bccd700da9db995e5333721577b2be0" :answer-field/choices ["q-76473/selection/ma-v1/ae1030de8cc55eaa89a7ff76b335d62f755394361c50f0224f5c7013158a0390/b331b72fbc647af25e87beeed0ecd2094bccd700da9db995e5333721577b2be0" "q-76473/selection/ma-v1/ae1030de8cc55eaa89a7ff76b335d62f755394361c50f0224f5c7013158a0390/3094eae62ed7931038a0e6b3203e28a7df4866da553ee53dc7578468910b0606" "q-76473/selection/ma-v1/ae1030de8cc55eaa89a7ff76b335d62f755394361c50f0224f5c7013158a0390/ab79d8e668398b9227c293e171d9553dc22cb5cf8447df9b53c5da8b7863542f" "q-76473/selection/ma-v1/ae1030de8cc55eaa89a7ff76b335d62f755394361c50f0224f5c7013158a0390/3f707b04d69b0b233940d68a127f5014b47a36c3a68a9502184d3bcef692b87d" "q-76473/selection/ma-v1/ae1030de8cc55eaa89a7ff76b335d62f755394361c50f0224f5c7013158a0390/0112f78b443cd401e4d0e83cb3b567685c157bc6e858b9f478baff3b065df996"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-76473"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/103c586778cdd8181fdc8396a92472df0525fc8e1c77fde9a7c3f731dca6df07.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $OABO,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup {-{C}_{2}}\\cup {C}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ is the same path as ${C}_{2}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-76473/selection/ma-v1/ae1030de8cc55eaa89a7ff76b335d62f755394361c50f0224f5c7013158a0390"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-39342"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2-t,t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t)\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=2-t$ and $y=t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2-t+t)\\,i+(2-t-t)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) \\\\  & =-2+(2-2t) \\\\  & =-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2-(1-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-77191/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "7cdc12b4-10ad-577d-bffa-821746256a2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77191/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer/id #uuid "d6c1ba85-ee74-5e78-944b-e912801c796e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77191/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "e2d034c2-6ca0-5097-a3ec-1886658f2040" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77191/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "e710d8cb-1dd7-549c-9e98-ee9fff60fdce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77191/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "f465457d-9597-5468-a5db-a7bf86b8a1d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77191/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e04d1706-e4ee-5f20-88dc-73e0f5bd2ff4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-77191/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-77191/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-77191/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" "q-77191/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-77191/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-77191/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-77191" :question/id #uuid "0ab1f56f-02fc-5f46-858a-7849e0bd1d9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-77191" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/0457cb819e6f39ef9c0978e356cff788e1cf5edbef9cbf844a054ab777b70eac.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of the line segment from the point $B(0,1)$ to the point $D(1,1).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-\\frac{3}{2},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x-2y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$  It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨-2,0⟩+t(⟨0,1⟩-⟨-2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨-2+2t,t⟩ \\\\  & =(-2+2t)i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=-2+2t$ and $y=t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =(x-2y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j \\\\  & =((-2+2t)-2t)\\,i+(t-(-2+2t))\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+(2-t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2+2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(-2\\,i+(2-t)\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i+j) \\\\  & =-2-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+(-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2-t)\\,\\text{d}t-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & ={[-2t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =[-2(1)-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0]-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-77191/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-77191"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-39673/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer/id #uuid "5e8c4b83-ba8a-5d16-bd8c-e5ed99be2ad3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39673/selection/087d6195df78ac19ad68ba3746305a749712648815fc499a6add393278e08471" :answer/id #uuid "2083c2e6-7d2a-5e0f-8192-a12055690202" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39673/selection/04ab8c59d12921ca2d995f79fd8be713086563c65c468afaa0d4bd8ea5bfffaf" :answer/id #uuid "bbe46702-0779-5802-ba7f-2b20370f0a6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39673/selection/a5221bf5219719264ce82e6834f570b4690cc4627bd43eb9432170bf0ca936cf" :answer/id #uuid "2224fb9e-af87-566e-a8ea-6a6ad44e203e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39673/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "a3b02fcc-a142-5e8b-a69a-0fd4ab1bf686" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39673/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b3ea1cae-7b0b-5f81-8c1f-98fa88663273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-39673/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-39673/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" "q-39673/selection/087d6195df78ac19ad68ba3746305a749712648815fc499a6add393278e08471" "q-39673/selection/04ab8c59d12921ca2d995f79fd8be713086563c65c468afaa0d4bd8ea5bfffaf" "q-39673/selection/a5221bf5219719264ce82e6834f570b4690cc4627bd43eb9432170bf0ca936cf" "q-39673/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39673" :question/id #uuid "ff3b2637-63d0-5a51-b3a4-46d0fad2a2ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-39673" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/73c6a8268a179f72611fb65e099d1249d48501e4fd4731d03747800e1944fd97.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,2)$ to the point $D(1,3).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{7}{2},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(2x-3y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$  It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩+t(⟨1,2⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,1+t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i+(1+t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=t$ and $y=1+t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-3(1+t))\\,i+(t+(1+t))\\,j \\\\  & =(-3-t)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =((-3-t)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =-3-t+2t+1 \\\\  & =t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-2t]}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}-2-0]+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-39673/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-39673"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-77180/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "c2ef356c-189b-5e99-a808-46697b531d81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77180/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "20553f41-4af2-5f54-b6a1-5a66c62ca370" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77180/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "278d4085-7e0a-578c-9e8a-148389aef44b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77180/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "34b747f3-40dc-55c1-b06f-5e2b43226b93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77180/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "5dd041e4-5c46-5b1b-932b-b7a7a12d0e0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77180/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cea72292-bb3b-5849-8e31-284ababca1a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-77180/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-77180/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-77180/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-77180/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-77180/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-77180/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-77180" :question/id #uuid "6b23e654-4079-5b0e-97ef-807a9ce88efc" :question/math-academy-id "q-77180" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/93c6184f39b28a057866bb02e11e5e81a7abea4ff8a9a907670dd30cfc93d557.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,1)$ to the point $O(0,0).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(2x+y)\\,i+(x-2y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment from $B$ to $O.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =b+t(0-b) \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩+t(⟨0,0⟩-⟨1,1⟩) \\\\  & =⟨1-t,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(1-t)\\,i+(1-t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=1-t$ and $y=1-t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(2x+y)\\,i+(x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2(1-t)+(1-t))\\,i+((1-t)-2(1-t))\\,j \\\\  & =(3-3t)\\,i+(-1+t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =((3-3t)\\,i+(-1+t)\\,j)\\cdot (-i-j) \\\\  & =-3+3t+1-t \\\\  & =-2+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+(-2t+{t}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2+(1-2) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-77180/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-77180"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-39674"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/512905447203d1d3a96781d20d586f9c4cf5cc9392fab50607b6fc77e9b00121.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,4),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,4)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\n${C}_{1}$ is the section of parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<2.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =t+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =[2+8]-2 \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9535"] :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (3x+2) \\: \\mathbf{i} - y^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be a path in $\\mathbb R^2.$ Write $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}$ in the form $\\displaystyle\\int_C P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x +  Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = P(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y) \\: \\text{d}x + Q(x,y) \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P = 3x+2$ and $Q = -y^3.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6319"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/38ab4af726729b5c9804f4aa13801d637e5e62eda085d6611174586827ef8c11.png)\n\nConsider the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $FGHF$ shown above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction. Express this integral as a sum of line integrals along $C_1, C_2,$ and $C_3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC = \\{-C_1\\} \\cup \\{-C_2\\} \\cup C_3,\n$$\n\nwhere $-C_1$ and $-C_2$ are the same paths as $C_1$ and $C_2$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6286"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/001487e4a94988a24d15702a18692e9deb2495a7cc7ec5c9f71b98fea13e0ce8.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $O(0,0),$ and the path $C_2$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = -4,$ evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x, y) = (2x-3y) \\,\\mathbf{i} + (2y-5x)\\,\\mathbf {j}$ and $C = C_1\\cup C_2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n+\n \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path $C_2$ is the line segment from $O$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =b+t(c-b) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\lt t \\lt 1.$ Along this segment, we have $x = t$ and $y = t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\,i+(2y-5x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-3t)\\,i+(2t-5t)\\,j \\\\  & =-t\\,i-3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(-t\\,i-3t\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =-t\\cdot 1+(-3t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+[-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4+[-2+0] \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
